A||||2||gkozlov||What wheels fit my Celebrity||||5||
Z||000000||gkozlov||09-30-2001||04:14 PM||||According to several posts I've read Fiero wheels fit the a 84 Chevy Celebrity.  I tried and they dont.  Does any body know the bolt pattern for the Celebrity?  I have a bent rim on my Celebrity and would like to know what cars to look at for replacement.  Any help would be appreciated.  Thanks<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">         <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A><BR>MSN MESSENGER: wkozlov@hotmail.com<BR>ICQ # 18515452||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rick 88||09-30-2001||07:57 PM||||Any GM A-Body from that era will have wheels that will fit. 6000, Ciera, and Century use the same pattern. Look for 6000 SE or STE/Century T-Type/or Ciera GT alloys for some nice styles.<P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||09-30-2001||08:02 PM||||There are two A car bolt patterns. The 5 x 100mm (same as the pattern used on the Fiero) and the 5 x 115mm heavy duty. <P>Depending on the year of the car it could have either, but the 660 V6 usually had the 100mm unless the HD suspension was ordered and the 90 degree V6's (3.3, 3.8) usually had 115 mm. The 3.0L usually came with the 100mm except with HD suspension excluding early years (82-85) or with the L4.<P>You have to measure and check. A-car's were all over the map. The larger 10.25" front rotors had 115mm, the 9.5" had 100mm. but if you can measure the rotors, you can measure the lug spacing.<P>It sounds like you have 115mm if the Fiero's won't fit (A-car offset is different too). Ciera GT/International's, 6000STE's, Buick T-Type's, Celebrity HO's all used 115mm.<P>Terry<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 09-30-2001).]||||reg||1
